Desktop game engines forgive a surprising amount of allocation sloppiness. Compile that exact same 3D scene to   WebAssembly and WebGL 2   for instant browser play on GitHub Pages, and every extra physics query, per-bullet node instantiation, or uncached shader variant immediately shows up as a dropped frame.

  [Horde Survival 3D](https://mohsen-niksirat.github.io/Horde Survival 3D Godot/)   is part of a personal game-engineering trilogy where I implemented wave-survival roguelite mechanics across   Godot 4 (GDScript / 3D Web)  ,   Unity (C )  , and   Android (Kotlin 2D)   to compare engine ergonomics under heavy entity counts. Getting the 3D Web build locked at 60 FPS required rethinking three subsystems: collision broadphase, object lifetimes, and WebGL material warm-up.



   Replacing Full 3D Physics Bodies with Spatial Hash Grids

In a horde roguelite, 250+ enemies converge continuously on the player while orbital blades, area pulses, and ricochet projectiles sweep through the crowd. Attaching a full  CharacterBody3D  with  move and slide()  to every enemy forces the 3D physics server to resolve thousands of convex capsule contacts per frame.

Because combat takes place on a mostly planar arena ($XZ$ plane with visual height variation), enemy separation and projectile hit detection only need 2D uniform spatial hashing with squared-distance checks:

